:name: ST NUCLEO-F401RE with the board view
:description: The board, your firmware, and a drawing of it in your browser.
:
: Run from the repository root:
:
:   renode --console board.resc
:
: then open http://localhost:8000
:
: By default it loads the setup self-test. To run your own program instead:
:
:   renode --console -e '$bin=@path/to/your.elf' -e 'i @board.resc'
:
: Single quotes, not double. In a shell, "$bin" is the shell's own variable
: and expands to nothing before Renode ever sees it, leaving `=@path/...`,
: which is not a command. Renode rejects it and abandons the whole line, so
: this script never runs and nothing is loaded at all. The only clue is
: `No such command or device: =`.
:
: This used to say the board came up running the self-test instead, without
: saying so. Measured on 1.16.1: it does not come up. That silent failure is
: real, but it belongs to a different mistake -- naming this script
: positionally. `renode board.resc -e '$bin=...'` sets the variable too late,
: and the board then does come up running the self-test rather than your
: firmware, reporting nothing.

$name?="NUCLEO-F401RE"

using sysbus
mach create $name

: board.repl, not boards/nucleo_f401re.repl. The first inherits the second and
: carries anything you added from the palette, so the board you drew and the
: board your code runs on are one file.
machine LoadPlatformDescription $ORIGIN/board.repl

$bin?=$ORIGIN/verify/verify.elf

macro reset
"""
    sysbus LoadELF $bin
"""

runMacro $reset

: No sensor is given a starting value here, because the board has no sensor on
: it: a real NUCLEO-F401RE carries none, so one is a part you add from the
: palette in the board view. Once you have added one, its reading is yours to
: set from the panel beside it.
:
: Whatever you set, it will read back slightly differently - 25 comes back as
: 24.99. See the 'sensor-value-quantisation' row of divergences.json.

i $ORIGIN/vis/vis_f401re.py

serveVisualization 8000

start
